        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "Propagate AbortSignal through bundle MCP tool/resource/prompt calls",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nWhen an agent run is cancelled, pending bundle MCP tool/resource/prompt requests keep running because the per-call `AbortSignal` is dropped after `AgentTool.execute`. This leaves MCP child work alive after the caller has aborted, wastes resources, and can produce late results that race with cleanup.\n\n## Why This Change Was Made\n\nPR #104110 fixed the same issue for bundle LSP JSON-RPC requests by forwarding the `AbortSignal` and emitting `$/cancelRequest`. Bundle MCP uses the same `AgentTool.execute` contract but ignores the signal all the way down to the MCP SDK client. This change plumbs the signal through the same layers so MCP requests can be cancelled.\n\n## User Impact\n\n- Cancelling an agent turn or session now stops pending bundle MCP tool calls, resource reads, and prompt fetches instead of letting them run to completion.\n- Reduces wasted compute and prevents late MCP responses from being applied after cancellation.\n\n## Evidence\n\n- Added a regression test in `src/agents/agent-bundle-mcp-tools.materialize.test.ts` that verifies the `AbortSignal` is forwarded from each projected tool's `execute` into `runtime.callTool`, `listResources`, `readResource`, `listPrompts`, and `getPrompt`.\n- Existing tests pass:\n  - `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs src/agents/agent-bundle-mcp-tools.materialize.test.ts src/agents/agent-bundle-mcp-runtime.test.ts` → 57 tests passed across 2 shards.\n  - `npx oxfmt --check src/agents/agent-bundle-mcp-materialize.ts src/agents/agent-bundle-mcp-runtime.ts src/agents/agent-bundle-mcp-types.ts src/agents/agent-bundle-mcp-tools.materialize.test.ts` → clean.\n  - `npx oxlint src/agents/agent-bundle-mcp-materialize.ts src/agents/agent-bundle-mcp-runtime.ts src/agents/agent-bundle-mcp-types.ts` → clean.\n- Minimal scope: only touches bundle MCP runtime/types/materialize + one focused test file.",
